                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "[code][url=https://store.steampowered.com/curator/32732116-IndieGems/]Follow our curator page: IndieGems if you like and want to see more reviews like this one. [/url][/code]\n\n\nThe Forgotton Void: is a combination between a puzzle game and a turn based battle game.\nIt is strange combination and it doesn't work that well.\n\nThere are two worlds: The Celestial World (humans live here) and The Null World (evil creatures live here). Two guardians were chosen to fix the balance between both worlds.\nYou are one of the guardians, but you lost your memory so you have no idea what to do.\n\nWhen you walk around in the world, you see a few houses. None of them can be entered. \nAll bridges have some kind of locks. They need to be opened. Here the puzzle game comes in: you have to draw a line between the starting point and the end point. The puzzles get more difficult along the way, but it always stays the same kind of puzzle.\nYou will arrive in a town where you can buy your equipment, potions, etc. There are a few NPCs you can talk to. And you can do a few quests.\n\nAfter that it becomes really frustrating, you enter a kind of void and you have to battle all the monsters. Those monsters are really strong and will poison you each turn. They only need to hit you twice and you are dead. The monsters only move when you move, but they all move towards you. You can dash, to try not to get surrounded by the monsters. Even then it is almost impossible to beat them all. You need mods and a lot of potions to beat 1 or 2 monsters and then there are still 6 left you have to beat.\nIf you die, you have to start over at the beginning of the void and all the monsters are there again.\n\nThe game has full controller support. The dev even advises to use a controller.\n\nThe price is nice. The graphics are okay. But because of the frustrating battle system, I could not recommend this game.\n\nDisclaimer: The copy of the game was provided by the publisher, however, it did not influence my opinion in any way.",
